    "result_text": "There is one test class (the acceptance suite), and it passes. The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the read-only `timezone` field on the Owner API, derived from the owner's locality/region via the fixed region-to-timezone table.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `timezone` property to the `Owner` schema (right after `locality`), documenting the IANA-name mapping. The `OwnerDto` is generated from this spec at build time.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../util/LocalityResolver.java`** \u2014 Added a `REGION_TIMEZONE` table (`NSW\u2192Australia/Sydney`, `VIC\u2192Australia/Melbourne`, `QLD\u2192Australia/Brisbane`) and a public `timezone(String region)` method returning the IANA name, or `null` for a region with no mapping (e.g. `UNKNOWN`). This keeps the region\u2194timezone table alongside the existing region tables.\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added a `@Mapping` for `timezone` and a `formatTimezone` method that reuses the existing `formatLocality` logic to resolve the region, then maps it through `LocalityResolver.timezone(...)`. This guarantees `timezone` is always consistent with the reported `locality`.\n\nThe acceptance test (`coreReturnsTimezoneForRegion`, which asserts a Sydney/NSW owner gets `locality: \"NSW\"` and `timezone: \"Australia/Sydney\"`) passes, and `BUILD SUCCESS` with no failures.",
